AWS-012 — Workflow Composition

Document ID: AWS-012
Title: Workflow Composition
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Workflow Composition Model (CWCoM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Workflow Composition Model establishes the principles, structures, interfaces, validation mechanisms, and governance required to construct complex engineering workflows from reusable Workflow Definitions while preserving modularity, determinism, interoperability, and engineering intent.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Workflow composition architecture
Reusable workflow modules
Hierarchical workflows
Workflow interfaces
Dependency management
Composition validation
Governance
Composition lifecycle

This specification applies to every composed Workflow Definition.

3. Canonical Definition

Workflow Composition is the governed assembly of two or more Workflow Definitions into a larger executable workflow while preserving the identity, autonomy, and semantics of each participating workflow.

Composition combines workflows.

It SHALL NOT alter the internal semantics of composed workflows.

4. Objectives

The Workflow Composition Model SHALL:

Enable workflow reuse.
Reduce duplication.
Preserve modularity.
Support hierarchical execution.
Enable scalable engineering programs.
Preserve governance.
Maintain traceability.
Enable interoperability.
5. Composition Principles

Every workflow composition SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Modularity

Every reusable workflow SHALL remain independently maintainable.

Semantic Preservation

Composition SHALL preserve the canonical meaning of every composed workflow.

Explicit Interfaces

Workflow interactions SHALL occur only through declared interfaces.

Implicit coupling SHALL NOT exist.

Identity Preservation

Each composed workflow SHALL retain its own identity and version.

Governed Composition

Composition SHALL comply with applicable Canonical Policy Objects.

Explainability

The composition structure SHALL be understandable by engineers and machines.

